Third-party software licences
================================================================================

This distribution is MIT. Most of what it installs is MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0, ISC
or PSF. Four dependencies are not, and a reader doing licence due diligence on
an MIT package should not have to discover them by installing it.

THE COPYLEFT DEPENDENCIES, AND WHAT PULLS EACH ONE IN

  svglib          LGPL-3.0-or-later
  python-bidi     LGPL (declared by classifier, no SPDX expression published)
    Both arrive through `xhtml2pdf`, which is the whole of the `[report]` extra
    -- `pip install "open-threat-model[report]"`, the documented way to get the
    premium PDF -- and which the `dev` extra also installs. Neither is reachable
    from a bare `pip install open-threat-model`.

  certifi         MPL-2.0
  pathspec        MPL-2.0
    Ordinary transitive dependencies of the HTTP and tooling stacks.

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN

  These are installed by pip as separate, independently replaceable packages.
  Nothing here is vendored, bundled, statically linked, or redistributed inside
  this project's own artifacts; the wheel and sdist on PyPI contain none of
  their code. MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft on the upstream files themselves.

  That is a statement of the facts, not a legal opinion, and this project offers
  none. Whether the arrangement is acceptable for a particular use -- especially
  vendoring, bundling into a single-file executable, or redistributing a
  container image to a customer, where the analysis is different -- is a
  decision for the reader and their counsel.

  No licence check runs in CI today, because the release SBOM currently records
  no Python components at all, so nothing automated would notice a new copyleft
  dependency arriving. `tests/test_p3_supply_third_party_licences.py` covers the
  gap in the meantime: it reads the installed distributions and fails if one
  declares a copyleft licence and is not named above.

UNCLASSIFIABLE LICENCE STRING (web UI dependency tree)

  The npm package `rgbcolor` declares "MIT OR SEE LICENSE IN FEEL-FREE.md",
  which is not a valid SPDX expression, so automated tooling cannot classify it
  and reports it as UNKNOWN. Recorded here as a known exception rather than left
  to surface as an unexplained scanner result.

================================================================================
Third-party frameworks, taxonomies and standards
================================================================================

This project maps generated threats to public security frameworks so that its
output is traceable. Understanding exactly what is redistributed matters, so it
is stated plainly:

WHAT IS REDISTRIBUTED
  Identifiers and short official names only -- for example "CWE-287",
  "CAPEC-151", "V2.2.1", "PR.AA-01", "A.8.24", "LLM01:2025", "AML.T0051".
  These are used as references, the way a bibliography cites a work.

WHAT IS NOT REDISTRIBUTED
  The normative text of any standard. No control descriptions, requirement
  text, questionnaire items or guidance are copied from the sources below. All
  descriptive prose shipped in this repository -- including every `notes` field
  in the framework map files and every heuristic description -- was written for
  this project.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR YOU
  A mapping here is a pointer for traceability. It is not a certified
  crosswalk, it is not endorsed by any of the organisations below, and it does
  not by itself demonstrate compliance with anything. Read the source.

